    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d100">

                <pb facs="0205" n="135" sameAs="frus:frus1862/pg135"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Adams.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 299.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-07-12"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >July</hi> 12, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of June 26 (No. 179) has been
                    received.</p>
                <p>You inform me that Mr. Dudley, our consul at Liverpool, has brought to your
                    notice a new and flagrant violation of neutrality which is being attempted in
                    some British port, and that you have remonstrated against it with the British
                    government, and also have called Captain Craven to Southampton to defeat the
                    enterprise. You, however, do not inform me of the name of the vessel; her
                    particular character or purpose, or any of the circumstances of the case. I have
                    communicated the imperfect information thus received to the Navy Department, in
                    the hope that it may be able to render it useful.</p>
                <p>This transaction will furnish you a suitable occasion for informing Earl Russell
                    that since the Oreto and other gunboats are being received by the insurgents
                    from Europe to renew demonstrations on our national commerce, Congress is about
                    to authorize the issue of letters of marque and reprisal, and that if we find it
                    necessary to suppress that piracy, we shall bring privateers into service for
                    that purpose, and, of course, for that purpose only.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
